<section>
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">
            <div class="col-lg-8" align="justify">
                <h3><b>Managing Change for Successful Manufacturing Automation Adoption</b></h3>
                <p>Automation changes how teams perform daily tasks. A structured change strategy helps users understand the new process and use automation effectively.</p>
                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Explain the Automation Vision: Show how automation reduces repetitive work and improves speed, consistency, and visibility.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Engage Process Owners: Involve production, operations, finance, procurement, quality, IT, and management stakeholders.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Provide Role-Based Training: Train users on automated workflows, approvals, exceptions, and new responsibilities.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Roll Out in Phases: Automate processes progressively and monitor results.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Provide Ongoing Support: Review automation performance and continuously optimize workflows.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul><br />

                <h3><b>How to Optimize Manufacturing Automation for Maximum ROI</b></h3>
                <p>Manufacturing automation should continuously evolve to reduce manual effort, improve process speed, and deliver measurable operational value.</p>
                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Define Automation KPIs: Measure processing time, manual effort, error rates, workflow volume, exceptions, and productivity.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Integrate Core Systems: Connect automation with ERP, production, inventory, quality, procurement, and analytics applications.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Improve User Experience: Simplify approvals, exception handling, and automated process interactions.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Review Automation Performance: Regularly assess workflow reliability, adoption, exceptions, and business outcomes.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Leverage Analytics: Use workflow analytics and reporting to identify additional automation opportunities.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="col-lg-4 Imgborder">
                <div><img src="~/assets/img/dynamics365/Developer2.webp" class="img-fluid text-start ms-0 mb-5 mt-3 me-3 p-3" alt="Manufacturing Automation" /></div>
